## =====================================================================
## USB PicoScope bring-up - DAQ analog out into a scope input
## =====================================================================
##
## The first thing to run on a rig that has never had a PicoScope on it.
## It drives ao0 on the PCIe-6351 from 0 to +5 V in 1 V steps and captures
## a trace on PicoScope channel A at every step. With ao0 wired to A it is
## SELF-CHECKING: trace N must read N volts, so a wrong range, a wrong
## scaling or a wrong channel is obvious without a meter.
##
##   >>> WIRE AO 0 TO PICOSCOPE CHANNEL A with a BNC cable before running.
##
##   >>> AND CHECK NOTHING ELSE IS ON ao0. This drives a real +5 V. If a
##   >>> galvo, piezo or amplifier is connected, change 'scan' and
##   >>> 'limits' to a safe span first.
##
## Without the cable it still runs and still proves the mechanics, but A
## floats and every trace reads near zero.
##
## WHAT THIS IS ACTUALLY TESTING, in order:
##
##   1. pscan can find and open the unit at all (the PicoSDK libraries are
##      installed, the cable is in, the serial is readable);
##   2. the range and coupling in this file reach the hardware;
##   3. the ADC counts in the .dat convert to the right volts through
##      scp.vgain - which is the one thing that is silently wrong if the
##      range handling is wrong;
##   4. a trace lands per coordinate, in step with the axis.
##
## HOW TO READ THIS FILE
##
## The scope block sits INSIDE the axis block, and that is what says it
## runs once per voltage:
##
##   for each of 6 ao0 levels          <- action d2a
##       capture one trace            <- action picoscope
##
## 6 x 1 = 6 traces. See examples/nested_loops.con for the general rules;
## the two that matter here are that only a loop (an axis block, or
## 'count') may contain other blocks, and that every recording block must
## sit at the same depth.
##
## Run it in this order:
##
##   pscan --dry-run examples/bringup_picoscope.con
##   pscan --timer   examples/bringup_picoscope.con
##
## --dry-run opens nothing and prints the nest, the grid and the output
## files. Do it first; it catches a typo in this file before anything
## drives 5 V. It also prints the line
##
##   This config NESTS its blocks, so the file decides the order
##
## which is how you confirm the hierarchy parsed as written rather than
## being read by the flat positional rule.
##
## WHAT THE OPENING LOG SHOULD SAY. There is no 'pydaq list' for a
## PicoScope - the first run IS the detection - so read these two lines
## before trusting anything after them:
##
##   -> PicoScope 5244D (serial GQ123/456), ps5000a driver 2.1.x, via
##      /opt/picoscope/lib/libps5000a.so.2
##   -> PicoScope armed: A at +/-5V, 1000 samples @ 1 MS/s (asked 1 MS/s),
##      1 capture(s)
##
## The model and serial are read off the unit, not out of this file, so
## they confirm WHICH box answered. The second line is the configuration
## that actually took: if the achieved rate differs from the asked rate,
## the unit has no such timebase and the delay axis in the .m is the
## achieved one.
##
## IF IT DOES NOT OPEN, the message says which of three things happened:
##
##   "no PicoSDK library could be loaded"  the driver packages are not
##       installed. Run 'bash install.sh' with the scope plugged in; see
##       README under OSCILLOSCOPES. Nothing to do with the cable.
##   "no PicoScope was found on USB"  the libraries are there and no unit
##       answered. Check the cable and the light. A 2000/3000 series unit
##       WITHOUT an 'A' in the model number uses a different PicoSDK
##       library and is not driven by pscan at all.
##   "N PicoScope units are attached"  more than one is plugged in, and
##       pscan refuses to guess which one you meant. It prints the exact
##       'series' and 'serial' lines to paste into the block below.
##
## --timer is the other half of the point: it breaks each trace down so
## you can see what a USB capture really costs per coordinate on this rig,
## rather than taking an estimate for it. Worth recording the numbers in
## this header once you have them, as the baseline to compare against if
## the scan ever feels slow.
##
## EXPECTED OUTPUT
##
##   bringup_picoscope_pico0.dat   6 traces x 1000 samples x int16 = 12000 bytes
##   bringup_picoscope_pico0.m     the geometry and the volts scaling
##
## There is no _trigtime.dat: this capture is untriggered, and a PicoScope
## writes no trigger-time sidecar even when it is triggered. See README.
##
## The .m should say points_per_trace=1000, n_traces=6, n_channels=1,
## multitraces=1 and n_averages=[1]. Check the data without leaving the
## shell - the mean of each trace should climb 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5:
##
##   python3 -c "
##   import re, numpy as np
##   m = open('bringup_picoscope_pico0.m').read()
##   g = float(re.search(r'scp\.vgain=\[([^,\]]+)', m).group(1))
##   o = float(re.search(r'scp\.voff=\[([^,\]]+)', m).group(1))
##   d = np.fromfile('bringup_picoscope_pico0.dat', dtype=np.int16).reshape(-1, 1000)
##   for i, t in enumerate(d):
##       v = g * t.astype(float) - o
##       print(f'{i:3d}  set {i:5.1f} V   read {v.mean():+8.4f} V   sd {v.std():.5f}')
##   "
##
## A reading that is a constant FACTOR out (every trace 2x or 0.5x) is a
## range problem. A reading that is right but noisy at the millivolt level
## is just 8-bit resolution on a +/-5 V range - 39 mV per count - which is
## why a real measurement uses the narrowest range that fits the signal.
##
## Reload in MATLAB exactly as a LeCroy run: same .dat layout, same .m
## fields. That is the point of the format being shared.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SWEEP: ao0 over 0..5 V in 1 V steps, 6 points.
##
## Deliberately 0..5 and not 0..10, so the whole sweep sits inside the
## +/-5 V scope range below and nothing has to be guessed about headroom.
##
## 'device Dev1' is whatever 'pydaq list' reported. 'restore' returns ao0
## to 0 V when this loop ends rather than leaving the card driving 5 V
## into the scope afterwards.
##
## Axis 98, not the default 99: 99 prints as "Z/piezo" in the log, which
## would be misleading here. In a nested file the number no longer decides
## the order - the nesting does - but it still names the axis in the log
## and is what 'restore' belongs to.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
action d2a
axis 98
backend daqmx
device Dev1
channel 0
units volts
scan 0.0 5.0 1.0
limits 0.0 5.0
settle 0.01
restore

    ## -----------------------------------------------------------------
    ## ONE CAPTURE AT EACH LEVEL. Inside the axis block, so it runs once
    ## per voltage; that is the whole of what the nesting says.
    ##
    ## Every setting is here because a USB scope has no front panel to
    ## inherit one from - that is the difference from 'action scope'.
    ##
    ## 'series'/'serial' are commented out: with one unit attached pscan
    ## finds it and names it in the log. Uncomment and paste in what the
    ## error prints if you ever have two plugged in.
    ##
    ## 'v_max 5.0' is the FULL SCALE in volts, so the range is +/-5 V.
    ## pscan picks the narrowest range the unit offers that contains it,
    ## and refuses rather than quietly widening if nothing fits. For a
    ## real photodiode measurement, drop this to the smallest value your
    ## signal fits inside: the range is the resolution.
    ##
    ## 'coupling dc' MATTERS HERE and is the classic bring-up trap: AC
    ## coupling removes exactly the DC level this file is trying to
    ## measure, so every trace would read ~0 V and look like a dead
    ## cable.
    ##
    ## 'trigger none' is deliberate, not an omission. A DC level has no
    ## edge to trigger on, so each capture starts the moment pscan asks
    ## for it. Writing it explicitly is what distinguishes "I meant
    ## untriggered" from "I forgot" - leaving the line out does the same
    ## thing but prints a config warning. For anything PULSED, see the
    ## block at the bottom.
    ##
    ## 1000 samples at 1 MS/s is a 1 ms record, far more than a DC level
    ## needs; it is sized so --timer reports something representative of
    ## a real capture rather than of an empty one.
    ## -----------------------------------------------------------------
    action picoscope
    ## series ps5000a
    ## serial GQ123/456
    channels A
    v_max 5.0
    coupling dc
    sample_rate 1e6
    n_samples 1000
    trigger none
    captures 1
    averages 1
    timeout 5.0
    end

end

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## WHERE 'count' WOULD GO, AND WHAT IT WOULD MEAN
##
## Two placements, two different experiments. Both give 24 traces; they
## are not the same measurement.
##
## AVERAGE EACH POINT - count inside the axis, around the scope block:
##
##   action d2a
##   axis 98
##   ...
##       action count
##       count 4
##           action picoscope
##           ...
##           end
##       end
##   end
##
## REPEAT THE WHOLE SWEEP - count outside the axis. For a bring-up this
## is the more useful of the two: it tells you whether trace N reads N
## volts EVERY time, which is the drift and repeatability question, and
## the four passes can be compared instead of averaged blindly:
##
##   action count
##   count 4
##       action d2a
##       axis 98
##       ...
##           action picoscope
##           ...
##           end
##       end
##   end
##
## Note that averaging inside the scope block is a third thing again:
## 'averages 100' means the UNIT captures 100 segments and pscan means
## them into one written row, which is faster than four pscan traces
## because the stage and the software never see them - but it also cannot
## be undone later, and it says nothing about drift.
##
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------
## NEXT, ONCE THE ABOVE READS 0..5 V CORRECTLY
##
## Replace the picoscope block with something like this for a real pulsed
## measurement. The nesting does not change.
##
##     action picoscope
##     channels A, B
##     v_max 0.2, 5.0          ## signal tight, monitor wide
##     coupling dc, dc
##     sample_rate 50e6
##     n_samples 5000
##     pretrigger 10           ## keep 10% of the record BEFORE the trigger
##     trigger B 1.5 rising    ## or 'trigger ext 1.5 rising' for EXT in
##     captures 1              ## segments WRITTEN per trace (scp.multitraces)
##     averages 100            ## segments AVERAGED into each row (scp.n_averages)
##     timeout 5.0             ## a missed trigger pads the trace and carries on
##     end
##
## Two things to know before trusting that one:
##
##   * captures and averages both take more than one capture per trace.
##     'captures' writes them all, as LeCroy Sequence mode does;
##     'averages' means them into one row. They compose, and the ceiling
##     is the unit's capture memory split that many ways - pscan asks and
##     refuses with the numbers rather than shortening the record.
##   * a trigger that never fires is a DROPPED trace, not a hang: after
##     'timeout' seconds the trace is padded, its index is recorded in
##     bringup_picoscope_dropped.txt, and the scan carries on. Ten in a
##     row aborts the run. If that file exists after a scan, read it
##     before analysing anything.
